{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"Yes—**when the fund’s structure and applicable relief permit it**. In an in-kind ETF redemption, the ETF class generally delivers a pro rata basket of portfolio securities to an authorized participant rather than selling those securities for cash. The RIC typically recognizes no gain or loss on the in-kind distribution under IRC §852(b)(6). That can reduce portfolio-level taxable gains and the resulting capital-gains distributions, benefiting **all classes sharing that portfolio**, including mutual-fund shareholders.\n\nThe benefit is indirect: it depends on the fund actually making in-kind redemptions and avoiding sales it otherwise would need to make. It does not erase gains already realized, guarantee lower distributions, or eliminate taxes on sales within the portfolio.\n\nThis is not automatic for every RIC. Shared ETF/mutual-fund classes require applicable SEC exemptive or other regulatory relief and compliance with its conditions; a fund cannot assume that relief applies merely because it offers both classes."}}
